{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3157","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3157","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3157\",\"parent_revision\":\"ab3d1f951eae43335e86d9a804e71b8e1f156a7e\",\"root_revision\":\"77e964a22e72850684596f9a105c1f7509aa56809c06dd7abae34bdbd0def1cc\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe read path reports sections that do not exist and returns truncated content, while storage holds the correct data. No data is lost; the reader misreports what the backend holds.\\n\\nObserved on #3152. The GitHub issue body has 4 top-level sections. The cache record has 9 section keys with complete content \u2014 fact_check is 28,744 characters and impact_radius is 26,700 characters, each holding everything its author wrote. backlog_view reports 39 to 46 sections for the same item and returns fragments. Sections named \\\"Claim H: build_issue_body single caller, create path only\\\", \\\"Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\\", \\\"Systems Inventory\\\" and roughly thirty others appear in the view and exist nowhere in storage.\\n\\nRoot cause, proven. operations.py line 2475 defines _SECTION_BOUNDARY_RE as a naive multiline regex matching any level-2 or level-3 heading line anywhere in a body. _build_sections_metadata at operations.py line 2169 uses it at line 2189 to enumerate an item's sections from the rendered body. It has no awareness of entry blocks, so a markdown heading that is legitimately part of an entry's content is read as a section boundary.\\n\\nparsing.extract_sections solves the same problem correctly by delegating to _split_body_h2, a marko-AST splitter whose _EntryDivBlock extension makes heading-shaped lines inside entry-block content invisible as boundaries. The two disagree on the same input. Given a body whose single wrapped entry contains two level-2 headings, the regex reports 4 sections and extract_sections reports 2.\\n\\nextract_sections' own docstring at parsing.py line 738 states that both callers \\\"share one structural section boundary decision instead of independently maintaining their own hand-rolled line scanners that can silently disagree\\\" and warns that a naive line-based scan \\\"misidentifies a heading-shaped line inside entry-block content as a real section boundary\\\". That unification reached extract_sections and never reached _build_sections_metadata.\\n\\nThis is the same defect class that #2956 and #2970 closed. Per this package's ARCHITECTURE.md, those issues found 126-plus unregistered unknown__ keys accumulated in production items, root-caused to four independently evolving section-naming surfaces with no single registry. _SECTION_BOUNDARY_RE is a fifth such surface that the unification missed: it mints section names from arbitrary heading text with no reference to section_registry at all.\\n\\nWhy this matters more than a display glitch. Grooming gates decide from backlog_view. groom/finalize.md's required-sections gate checks presence and minimum content by section name. An agent deciding whether an item is ready reads the same view. A reader that invents sections and truncates content makes those decisions on data that does not exist \u2014 structurally the same failure as #3152 itself, one layer up. It also silently defeats sections= filtering, map=True and navigate=, which are the mechanisms agents rely on to read large items.\\n\\nScope of the fix. Route _build_sections_metadata's boundary detection through the same guarded structural splitter extract_sections uses, so the two cannot disagree. Level-3 section support must survive, since _build_sections_metadata matches both level-2 and level-3 sections. _SECTION_BOUNDARY_RE has other callers, including _slice_body_by_header_indices at operations.py line 2478 which takes a list of regex match objects; every reference must be handled coherently rather than patched around. Filtering semantics for show, since and section must not change.\\n\\nCORRECTION OF THIS ITEM'S ORIGINAL FILING. This item was first filed as \\\"backlog_groom reports success while discarding the content it was given, and a section with unbalanced entry tags absorbs the next section\\\", at P0, on the premise that three grooming writes had destroyed their content. That premise was false and is withdrawn in full. Every write persisted correctly and every groomed_updated true return was accurate. The reported data loss was the read path misreporting intact storage. The original filing also described a mutation queue growing to 75 pending entries as evidence of corruption; per ARCHITECTURE.md's storage-ownership section that queue is the documented durable pending-write path used while a remote provider is unreachable, and its growth is expected behaviour rather than a defect signal. Two real but unrelated defects found during that investigation \u2014 wrap_entry double-wrapping already-wrapped content, and the since filter raising ValueError on the zero-date fallback id \u2014 are fixed separately in PR #3160 and were never the cause of anything observed here.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_groom reports success while discarding the content it was given, and a section with unbalanced entry tags absorbs the next section** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-23, three live content-loss events while grooming #3152\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-22T15:31:08.043432Z</sub>\\n\\nCORRECTION 2026-08-23 \u2014 the root cause stated in this item's description is wrong, and its Part 2 reproduction does not demonstrate the observed failure.\\n\\nWhat was actually observed on #3152. The item now carries 46 top-level sections. Sections named \\\"Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\\", \\\"Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\\", \\\"Documentation (will become stale)\\\", \\\"Systems Inventory\\\" and \\\"Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\\" are the impact-analyst agent's level-3 subheadings promoted to top level. Roughly 28 sections named \\\"Claim A:\\\" through \\\"Claim I:\\\" are the fact-checker's subheadings, repeated across its three write attempts. The damage is section explosion, not truncation: content was split on its own markdown headings, and the fragment that appeared to survive in each section was simply the text preceding the first level-3 heading.\\n\\nWhy the original description got it wrong. The reproduction in Part 2 was constructed to match the hypothesis \u2014 nested entry wrappers with no headings \u2014 instead of using the agents' actual submitted content, which contained both nesting and level-3 headings. It reproduced a real but different defect and proved nothing about the observed failure.\\n\\nWhat cannot be reproduced against current source. No combination tried produces the explosion: wrapped entry content containing level-3 headings through extract_sections; the same with level-2 headings; pre-wrapped nested content containing level-3 headings; and the render_issue_body to parse_issue_body round trip with a level-3 heading inside an entry. The _EntryDivBlock guard inside _split_body_h2 holds in every case. That guard landed in commit bb8ee0c0a, \\\"unify section-key normalization between local writes and GitHub-parsed sections (#2964)\\\", dated 2026-08-18, and it is on main.\\n\\nLeading hypothesis, not established. extract_sections' own docstring records that the marko-AST splitter replaced a naive line-based scan which misidentified heading-shaped lines inside entry-block content as section boundaries \u2014 precisely the observed behaviour. The live failures came through the MCP server, which runs a cached build rather than working-tree source. So the explosion may be a stale-cache artifact of code predating bb8ee0c0a. This is under investigation. It is not yet confirmed, and the running server's actual revision has not been determined; the plugin cache was not found under the expected path.\\n\\nWhat in this item survives the correction. Part 1 stands unchanged and is independent of the cause of the explosion: every one of the three destructive writes returned groomed_updated true, and no part of the return value let a caller distinguish persisting the given content from persisting a fragment of it from persisting none of it. That violates the repo's silent-failure-prevention rule at its most consequential point and is worth fixing regardless of what caused the loss.\\n\\nPart 2 must be re-scoped. If the parser boundary defect is not reachable in current source, this item's remaining scope is the write-path reporting contract plus, separately, cleaning the 40-plus junk sections now sitting on #3152.\\n\\nTwo defects found while investigating this are already fixed on branch fix/3152-no-generated-story-acceptance-criteria, commit 957194655, and are in PR #3160. Neither caused the observed explosion. First, wrap_entry double-wrapped content that already carried an entry wrapper, producing a leaked wrapper inside entry content and an orphan entry with an empty id. Second, the documented since filter raised ValueError \\\"year 0 is out of range\\\" on any section whose entries fell back to the zero-date id, making that filter unusable on legacy unwrapped content.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"1ad6b3db6fb0f5ef802bc7f9b01f61bb7fbb3ad83049089fb175dfd6f90f58b2\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQOIGQA\"}"}